      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Cook the rice and keep warm. Pound each pork steak to about 1cm thickness. Season lightly with salt."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Set up a breading station: cornflour in one shallow dish, 2 beaten eggs in another, and"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "panko"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "breadcrumbs in a third. Coat each pork steak in cornflour, then egg, then panko. Press the crumbs on firmly."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the vegetable oil in a deep frying pan to 170°C. Fry the pork in 2 batches for 3 to 4 minutes per side until golden and cooked through. Drain on a wire rack. Cut each cutlet into 2cm strips."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pour the dashi stock, soy sauce, mirin, and sugar into a wide frying pan. Bring to a simmer over medium heat. Add the spring onions and cook for 2 minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Lay the sliced pork cutlets in the simmering broth. Pour the 3 beaten eggs evenly over the top. Cover with a lid and cook for 1 to 2 minutes until the egg is just set but still soft."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Divide the rice between 4 bowls. Slide a portion of katsu and egg over each bowl. Spoon over the remaining broth and serve immediately."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "Panko coating falls off during frying",
              "cause": "The breading was not pressed on firmly enough, or the oil was not hot enough.",
              "fix": "Press the panko crumbs on with your palm and let the breaded cutlets rest for 5 minutes before frying. Fry at 170°C. Cold oil leads to a soggy, lifting coating."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Cutlet is soggy after the egg simmer",
              "cause": "The cutlets were left in the broth too long.",
              "fix": "The simmering step should take no more than 2 minutes. Serve as soon as the egg sets. The panko will soften slightly. That is correct for this dish, but it should not be waterlogged."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Pork is dry and tough inside",
              "cause": "The cutlets were too thick or overcooked during frying.",
              "fix": "Pound to a consistent 1cm thickness before breading. At 170°C a 1cm cutlet needs 3 to 4 minutes per side. Cut into one to check before serving."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Katsudon was created in Tokyo at the turn of the 20th century. It became a dish associated with success in Japan, eaten by students before exams because the word 'katsu' also means 'to win'. It is found in school canteens, family restaurants, and at home across the whole country. The dish has regional variations but the core of fried pork and soft egg on rice remains constant."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
